<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="A7.F17.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
          
<div id="A7.F17.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A7.F17.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="222.09" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 222.09" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,222.09) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 204.04 C 0 207.3 2.64 209.94 5.91 209.94 L 223.23 209.94 C 226.49 209.94 229.14 207.3 229.14 204.04 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 204.04 C 1.97 206.21 3.73 207.98 5.91 207.98 L 223.23 207.98 C 225.41 207.98 227.17 206.21 227.17 204.04 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 199.94)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 277.62 22.14 C 279.25 22.14 280.57 20.82 280.57 19.19 L 280.57 2.95 C 280.57 1.32 279.25 0 277.62 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 277.62 22.14 C 279.25 22.14 280.57 20.82 280.57 19.19 L 280.57 2.95 C 280.57 1.32 279.25 0 277.62 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:18.74em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="259.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Math Tutoring Simulator-AI Conversation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.43em;--ltx-fo-height:12.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="176.42" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 176.42)" width="185.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:13.43em;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Math Problem: The harmonic mean of two positive integers is the reciprocal of the arithmetic mean of their reciprocals. For how many ordered pairs (x,y) of positive integers is the harmonic mean of x and y equal to 20?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.3.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">User Turn 3: Okay, I see that \((x - 5)(y - 5) = 25\) is easier to work with. So, I need to find pairs of integers \(a\) and \(b\) such that \((a - 5)(b - 5) = 25\). I think I need help figuring out all the integer pairs that multiply to 25. Can you help me list them out?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.6.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">AI Tutor Turn 3: Certainly! To find integer pairs \((a, b)\) such that \(ab = 25\), we can list all the factor pairs of 25. Remember, since \(a\) and \(b\) can be positive or negative, we should consider both positive and negative factors. Here are the factor pairs: 1. \(a = 1\), \(b = 25\) 2. \(a = 25\), \(b = 1\) 3. \(a = -1\), \(b = -25\) 4. \(a = -25\), \(b = -1\) 5. \(a = 5\), \(b = 5\) 6. \(a = -5\), \(b = -5\) Now, recall that these pairs \((a, b)\) are actually \((x-5, y-5)\) from your factored equation \((x-5)(y-5) = 25\). To find the values of \(x\) and \(y\), you need to add 5 to each element of the pairs. Can you calculate the corresponding values of \(x\) and \(y\) for each pair?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.8.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">User Turn 4: Okay, I will add 5 to each of the factor pairs. Here’s what I get: 1. For \((1, 25)\), \(x = 1 + 5 = 6\), \(y = 25 + 5 = 30\) 2. For \((25, 1)\), \(x = 25 + 5 = 30\), \(y = 1 + 5 = 6\) 3. For \((-1, -25)\), \(x = -1 + 5 = 4\), \(y = -25 + 5 = -20\) 4. For \((-25, -1)\), \(x = -25 + 5 = -20\), \(y = -1 + 5 = 4\) 5. For \((5, 5)\), \(x = 5 + 5 = 10\), \(y = 5 + 5 = 10\) 6. For \((-5, -5)\), \(x = -5 + 5 = 0\), \(y = -5 + 5 = 0\) It looks like some of these pairs result in negative or zero values, which we can’t use since \(x\) and \(y\) are supposed to be positive integers. So, we’re left with: - \((6, 30)\) - \((30, 6)\) - \((10, 10)\) Does this look correct? Are these the only valid pairs for \(x\) and \(y\)?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.10.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">AI Tutor Turn 4: Yes, you’ve done that perfectly! You correctly identified and excluded the pairs where either \(x\) or \(y\) is not a positive integer. The valid pairs of positive integers \((x, y)\) that satisfy the condition where the harmonic mean is 20 are indeed: - \((6, 30)\) - \((30, 6)\) - \((10, 10)\) These are the only valid pairs given the constraints of the problem (positive integers). You’ve successfully found all the ordered pairs where the harmonic mean of \(x\) and \(y\) equals 20. Great job!</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p1.pic1.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
        
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="A7.F17.3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
          
<div id="A7.F17.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A7.F17.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="278.12" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 278.12" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,278.12) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 260.08 C 0 263.34 2.64 265.98 5.91 265.98 L 223.23 265.98 C 226.49 265.98 229.14 263.34 229.14 260.08 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 260.08 C 1.97 262.25 3.73 264.02 5.91 264.02 L 223.23 264.02 C 225.41 264.02 227.17 262.25 227.17 260.08 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 255.98)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 262.97 22.14 C 264.6 22.14 265.92 20.82 265.92 19.19 L 265.92 2.95 C 265.92 1.32 264.6 0 262.97 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 262.97 22.14 C 264.6 22.14 265.92 20.82 265.92 19.19 L 265.92 2.95 C 265.92 1.32 264.6 0 262.97 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:17.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="244.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Math Tutoring Human-AI Conversation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.43em;--ltx-fo-height:16.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="232.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 232.46)" width="185.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:13.43em;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.2.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Math Problem: The harmonic mean of two positive integers is the reciprocal of the arithmetic mean of their reciprocals. For how many ordered pairs (x,y) of positive integers is the harmonic mean of x and y equal to 20?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.4.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">User Turn 4: what is a reciprocal in this context?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.6.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">AI Tutor Turn 4: In this context, the reciprocal of a number \(x\) is \(\frac{1}{x}\). It’s essentially the value you get when you divide 1 by that number. For example, the reciprocal of 2 is \(\frac{1}{2}\), and the reciprocal of 5 is \(\frac{1}{5}\). When we are dealing with the equation \(\frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10}\), we are looking for values of \(x\) and \(y\) such that when you take the reciprocal of each and add them together, the sum equals \(\frac{1}{10}\). This means you need to find pairs of numbers \(x\) and \(y\) where the sum of their reciprocals equals \(\frac{1}{10}\). This involves a bit of number sense and sometimes trial and error with different values to see which pairs work. Would you like to try finding some pairs that might satisfy this condition?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">User Turn 5: 5 and 15?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.10.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">AI Tutor Turn 5: Great choice! Let’s check if the pair \( (x, y) = (5, 15) \) works. We need to verify if the sum of the reciprocals of 5 and 15 equals \( \frac{1}{10} \): \[ \frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{15} \] First, find a common denominator for the fractions: \[ \frac{1}{5} = \frac{3}{15} \] Now add the fractions: \[ \frac{3}{15} + \frac{1}{15} = \frac{4}{15} \] However, \( \frac{4}{15} \) is not equal to \( \frac{1}{10} \). So, unfortunately, the pair \( (5, 15) \) does not satisfy the condition \( \frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} \). Would you like to try another pair, or need help finding pairs that work?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.12.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">User Turn 6: Yes, help me.</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.13.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:21.1pt;"> </span><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.14.1.4" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:113.2pt;">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">AI Tutor Turn 6: Sure, let’s find some pairs that satisfy the equation \(\frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10}\). To do this, we can think about values of \(x\) and \(y\) that are factors of numbers related to 10, since their reciprocals need to sum to \(\frac{1}{10}\). We can start by setting \(x\) to some values and solving for \(y\). Let’s start with \(x = 10\): \[ \frac{1}{10} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} \] \[ \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} - \frac{1}{10} = 0 \] This doesn’t work since \(\frac{1}{y}\) cannot be zero. Let’s try \(x = 20\): \[ \frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} \] \[ \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} - \frac{1}{20} \] \[ \frac{1}{y} = \frac{2}{20} - \frac{1}{20} = \frac{1}{20} \] \[ y = 20 \] So, \( (20, 20) \) is a pair that works. Let’s try another value, \(x = 15\): \[ \frac{1}{15} + \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} \] \[ \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{10} - \frac{1}{15} \] \[ \frac{1}{y} = \frac{3}{30} - \frac{2}{30} = \frac{1}{30} \] \[ y = 30 \] So, \( (15, 30) \) and \( (30, 15) \) are pairs that work. We can continue this process to find more pairs. Would you like to try another value of \(x\) or need further explanation on how to proceed?</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p" style="width:134.3pt;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_verbatim ltx_inline-block" style="width:134.3pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="A7.F17.p2.pic1.2.15.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_pruned_first ltx_align_top" style="width:134.3pt;"></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
        
</div></div></div>
